CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure shows six panels labeled a) through f) illustrating the hierarchical windowed self-attention partitioning scheme used in the Swin Transformer. Panel a) shows the original photograph as the reference. In the remaining panels the partitions are shown as the photograph cut into slightly separated tiles with thin white gaps between them, each panel drawn with a slight 3D tilt: panels b) and c) show the image divided into 4×4 non-overlapping windows and the same 4×4 grid offset (shifted) by half a window width and height; panels d) and e) show 2×2 windows and their offset variant; and panel f) shows the degenerate 1×1 case in which the entire image is treated as a single attention region (panel captions read '4×4 windows', '4×4 offset windows', '2×2 windows', '2×2 offset windows', '1×1 window'). The figure demonstrates the Swin Transformer's shifted-window strategy: by alternating between regular and offset window partitions across successive transformer layers, the model enables information to flow across window boundaries without the quadratic cost of global self-attention.

PROJECTION TYPE: oblique
Call setCameraView({ projection: 'oblique', obliqueAngleDeg: 45, depthScale: 0.5, heightFraction }) after all geometry is added — a standard cabinet-oblique pose; the plan does not measure per-figure oblique angles. The scaffold implements oblique as a shear of the eye-space projection matrix, so the front face stays undistorted, depth edges leave at obliqueAngleDeg, and labels track the shear automatically. Build the TRUE 3D object — the shear is the camera's job. Do NOT shear geometry, do NOT wrap the figure in a sheared parent Object3D, and do NOT disable automatic matrix updates. The scaffold also provides the reveal interaction: dragging eases the shear out to expose the real 3D shape and a toggle button returns to the printed view — do not implement either yourself.
AXIS ASSIGNMENT: the pose is pinned, so in BoxGeometry(w, h, d) the w axis is screen-right, h is screen-up, and d is the receding diagonal. Before writing each BoxGeometry, look at the source image and decide which of the object's dimensions actually recedes there — do not assume it is the one the domain calls "depth". In feature-map / layer-stack diagrams the channel count is usually the w (horizontal) extent along the flow direction and a spatial dimension is d. Sanity-check the silhouette against the source: a block that should read as a long bar pointing at its neighbours must be long in w, not in d, or it will render as a sliver shooting into the page. If GEOMETRY NOTES assigns the axes in a way that contradicts the source image's silhouette, follow the image.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Single photographic image plane (graffiti mural: pink-faced character with black bob hair, yellow shirt, purple/lilac arms, white sign with red lettering, grey concrete wall, green foliage strip along the top, pale kerb strip along the bottom)
  - Sheared parallelogram outline of the image plane (vertical left/right edges, top and bottom edges slanting up-right), with a thin black border
  - Fine token grid: 8×8 thin dark lines overlaid on the whole image, present in every partitioned panel
  - Window partition tiles: the image cut into k×k sub-images separated by thick white gaps (k = 1, 2 or 4)
  - Offset (shifted) partition variant: the same window lattice displaced by half a window in both x and y, producing narrow half-width strips at all four edges
  - Panel caption text below the figure ('Original image', '4×4 windows', '4×4 offset windows', '2×2 windows', '2×2 offset windows', '1×1 window')
  - Panel letter label in the upper-left corner ('a)' … 'f)')
  - Highlight overlay for a selected window tile (brightened tile, dimmed remainder)
  - Self-attention connection lines drawn between all token centres inside the selected window tile
  - Contrast highlight for a neighbouring tile showing its attention lines never cross the shared boundary
  - Dashed boundary marker showing which token columns/rows were split apart in the regular grid and re-united in the offset grid

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
One image plane only (panels are reached through the slider/toggle, not drawn side by side). Plane lies in the X–Y plane: 3.2 world units wide (X) × 3.0 units tall (Y), sheared so the top edge is displaced +0.85 units in +X relative to the bottom edge (left and right edges stay vertical, top/bottom edges slant up to the right at ≈16° above horizontal) — exactly the parallelogram shape of every panel in the source. Depth (Z) is essentially zero: tiles are flat quads, lifted only ~0.02 units apart so gaps read cleanly. Token grid: 8×8 cells, thin dark grey lines (#3a3a3a, ~1px). Window gaps: white (#ffffff) strips of width ≈0.055 units (≈2% of plane width), drawn between tiles so each k×k tile is (3.2/k − gap) wide. Regular partition: boundaries at multiples of 1/k of the plane. Offset partition: boundaries shifted by half a window in both X and Y, so narrow half-width strips appear along all four edges (k=2 → 3×3 unequal tiles; k=4 → 5×5 unequal tiles). Photograph palette: grey concrete wall #b6b8bb, sky/foliage strip along the top (green #6f8a3c, ochre trim #b89a3c), character skin pink #efb3c4, black bob hair #1c1c1c, yellow shirt #e8b81f, lilac arms/shorts #c9a8d4, white sign #f2f2f2 with red graffiti lettering #e03c3c, pale kerb band #d9d4c8 along the bottom sixth. Panel letter label in upper-left, caption text centred below the plane, both in dark grey #222 sans-serif. Highlighted tile: full brightness with a 0.03-unit accent border #ff9d2e; unselected tiles dimmed to 45% opacity. Attention lines: thin translucent orange (#ff9d2e, alpha 0.35) connecting every pair of token centres inside the selected tile only.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "windowSize",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Windows per side (1 → 2 → 4)",
    "range": [
      1,
      4,
      1
    ],
    "default": 1,
    "teaches": "Attention is computed inside each window only, so shrinking the windows multiplies their number while shrinking each one's receptive field — 1×1 is full global attention, 4×4 is the cheapest, most local partition."
  },
  {
    "id": "offsetWindows",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Window partition",
    "options": [
      "Regular",
      "Offset (shifted by ½ window)"
    ],
    "default": "Regular",
    "teaches": "Shifting the lattice by half a window places token pairs that straddled a boundary into one shared window, so information crosses window borders between successive layers."
  },
  {
    "id": "selectWindow",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "Self-attention connections are confined to the tokens of a single window; a neighbouring tile's connections never reach across the white gap, making the locality constraint concrete."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "a) Original image",
    "narration": "The full photograph, uncut, with its fine token grid: every patch is a token that could in principle attend to every other.",
    "state": {
      "windowSize": 1,
      "offsetWindows": "Regular"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "f) 1×1 window",
    "narration": "With a single window, attention is global — one tile holds all 64 tokens. Click the tile to see the dense all-to-all connections and the cost they imply.",
    "state": {
      "windowSize": 1,
      "offsetWindows": "Regular"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "d) 2×2 windows",
    "narration": "The image splits into four windows separated by white gaps. Attention now runs only within each quadrant; tokens on either side of a gap cannot talk.",
    "state": {
      "windowSize": 2,
      "offsetWindows": "Regular"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "e) 2×2 offset windows",
    "narration": "Shifting the same lattice by half a window re-partitions the image: tokens that were split by the central boundary now sit together, so the next layer mixes across the old borders.",
    "state": {
      "windowSize": 2,
      "offsetWindows": "Offset (shifted by ½ window)"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "b) 4×4 windows",
    "narration": "Sixteen small windows: attention is highly local and cheap, but each token sees only a 2×2 token neighbourhood.",
    "state": {
      "windowSize": 4,
      "offsetWindows": "Regular"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "c) 4×4 offset windows",
    "narration": "The offset 4×4 grid leaves half-width strips at the edges and re-couples every boundary pair — alternating b) and c) across layers propagates information globally without global attention.",
    "state": {
      "windowSize": 4,
      "offsetWindows": "Offset (shifted by ½ window)"
    }
  }
]

AXIS ANGLES: null — null means the source is a flat 2D figure; pass axisScreenAngles: null to setCameraView for a front-on view.

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.68
}

NOTES: Render the photograph as a single texture; cut windows by drawing k×k (or the offset (k+1)×(k+1)) PlaneGeometry quads each with adjusted UV ranges so the mural stays continuous across tiles — never re-scale the texture per tile. Apply the oblique shear with a fixed matrix on the group (shearX proportional to Y) rather than a rotated perspective camera; use an orthographic camera looking down −Z so the parallelogram outline stays exact and no perspective convergence appears. Slider snaps to integer values 1, 2, 4 (skip 3) and cross-fades gap width; animate the offset toggle as a half-window translation of the boundary set over ~400 ms so the learner sees tiles re-form around the old seams. Token grid drawn as a LineSegments overlay clipped per tile. Click picking via raycast against the tile quads; attention lines built as a single BufferGeometry LineSegments rebuilt on selection, capped at the 64-token case. Labels and captions as CSS2DRenderer/HTML overlays so text is not sheared.

Follow the interaction plan above.

